A complete answer:
* gives three equations with domains, e.g. polynomial $h_1(x)$ on $[0,x_1]$, sinusoid $h_2(x)=a\sin[k(x-d)]+c$ on $[x_1,x_2]$ with $x_2-x_1\ge2\cdot\frac{2\pi}{k}$, rational $h_3(x)$ on $[x_2,x_3]$;
* checks $h_1(x_1)=h_2(x_1)$ and $h_2(x_2)=h_3(x_2)$;
* estimates each rate of change at the joins with small intervals, e.g. $\frac{h(x_1+0.001)-h(x_1)}{0.001}$, and shows $\frac{|r_1-r_2|}{|r_1|}\le10\%$.

A useful strategy: join at points where the sinusoid has a known slope (e.g. a maximum, where the rate is 0, matching a polynomial with a local max there).
